The entire architecture rests on one insight:

Fluids, structures, and waves are not different systems — they are different regimes of one field.

So we define a field:

[ \phi(x,t) ]

with four governing operators:

Inertia — (\mathcal{M}(\phi))

Damping — (\mathcal{D}(\phi))

Stiffness — (\mathcal{K}(\phi))

Forcing — (\mathcal{F}(\phi,\nabla\phi,x,t))

Unified evolution:

[ \mathcal{M}(\phi)\frac{\partial^2\phi}{t^2}

\mathcal{D}(\phi)\frac{\partial\phi}{t}

\mathcal{K}(\phi)\phi = \mathcal{F}(\phi,\nabla\phi,x,t) ]

Regimes are parameter sets:

Fluid: (\mathcal{D} \gg \mathcal{K})

Solid: (\mathcal{K} \gg \mathcal{D})

Wave: (\mathcal{M},\mathcal{K}) dominate

